#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542271} :activity/id #uuid "f0e0b381-c4f7-544d-803f-1f89f6490699" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049739 :topic/id #uuid "0fe4fee1-477c-5881-86c7-1921fd5fff4e" :topic/math-academy-id 3860} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542238 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542182 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2010fc61-4364-5862-a96d-774075019bbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-K{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541487} {:answer/id #uuid "705dbc1d-a754-523f-92e0-8940c3b04884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541650} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1b73b6-ad80-5c74-ab55-18bad8b8f274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-Kr" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541858} {:answer/id #uuid "d69ff15d-ef03-5444-8c1a-9ce4ef60002c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{K}{r}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541881} {:answer/id #uuid "f9f93573-3c61-5ff8-b156-76483ae8624a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{K}{{r}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541946}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541487} :answer-field/id #uuid "e60fe765-3ba7-5b2e-a3cc-67f6b659f5d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542082}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a8d62c5b-3e1c-59af-b194-160b1241f14c" :question/math-academy-id "q-143132" :question/problem "The area of a circle decreases at a rate that's proportional to the cube of its radius. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ in this process. Assume that $K>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **area** of a circle **decreases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to the cube of its **radius**. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *area $A$ of a circle* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ *decreases* at a rate that's *proportional* to the cube of its *radius.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-k{r}^{3}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe area of a circle is given by \n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\pi r\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-k{r}^{3} \\\\ 2\\pi r\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-k{r}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{k{r}^{3}}{2\\pi r} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{k{r}^{2}}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{2\\pi }>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-K{r}^{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1a243f40-c3c6-58d7-bac3-52d3bb082cf4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542255}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542243 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542145 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53f1b9c6-6af0-5a2a-af00-95a3f317bc35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{40}{|y|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541592} {:answer/id #uuid "6004d047-d373-53e3-95ab-5c21565957f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{10}{|x|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541617} {:answer/id #uuid "69b6b7e0-47f8-5d3f-90c7-9cc539874ad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{5}{|x|}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541634} {:answer/id #uuid "98ba0312-7934-5cd5-a8d0-964450104886" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{5|y|}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541739} {:answer/id #uuid "a9291194-9f4c-5ec3-89de-d6aac381c602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{|x|}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541772}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541617} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fa9844e-bf08-5151-a0ef-3ca82c6b8c21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542016}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "500cc9b6-df77-5d16-8dd2-a74988fb81dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-143140" :question/problem "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **inversely** proportional to the shortest distance from $P$ to the $y$-axis. The derivative at the point $(2,8)$ equals $5.$ What differential equation describes this curve?" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **inversely proportional** to the **shortest distance from $P$ to the $y$-axis**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *shortest distance from $P$ to the $y$-axis* is given by $|x|.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *inversely proportional* to the shortest distance from $P$ to the $y$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=k\\cdot \\frac{1}{|x|}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{k}{|x|}.\n$$\n\nIn addition, we're told that ${y}^{′}(x)=5$ when at the point $(2,8).$ We can use this to determine the value of the coefficient $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5=\\frac{k}{|2|}\\,⟹\\,k=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the differential equation describing the curve is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{10}{|x|}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2b8f298c-89ca-5efd-8419-026734b35b16"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542255 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373862 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2c4829e-9ff5-5c77-8160-757948c34286" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=3|x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373864} {:answer/id #uuid "7e16602c-ea2a-5cbc-a3ed-6fcf2ad9b012" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=8|x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373865} {:answer/id #uuid "80b53aba-6c00-50c6-bef0-87cc4e4f247b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=4\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373866} {:answer/id #uuid "4c0ceb4b-3c4e-561d-b09c-109e90eae4ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{24|y|}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373867} {:answer/id #uuid "a9af8465-e86d-58d5-908e-3721348d588c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373868}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373865} :answer-field/id #uuid "81b73e2e-d50c-5e49-8505-3ee73aad8826" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e6b89f7e-afce-55f3-8c93-dbf9b7119d01" :question/math-academy-id "q-143139" :question/problem "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ is proportional to the shortest distance from $P$ to the $y$-axis. The derivative at the point $(3,-5)$ equals $24.$ What differential equation describes this curve?" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **proportional** to the **shortest distance from $P$ to the $y$-axis**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *shortest distance from $P$ to the $y$-axis* is given by $|x|.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *proportional to* the shortest distance from $P$ to the $y$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=k\\cdot |x|\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=k|x|.\n$$\n\nIn addition, we're told that ${y}^{′}(x)=24$ when at the point $(3,-5).$ We can use this to determine the value of the coefficient $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24=k|3|\\,⟹\\,k=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the differential equation describing the curve is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=8|x|.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "52ea8b3b-19b7-5968-bcaa-348c34bcb384" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542243}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542256 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542180 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "031ea96a-3096-54b0-ad2a-4f96e9d8183b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=k-h" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541431} {:answer/id #uuid "25636b1a-6d91-597e-9b19-e787394d985e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-kh" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541499} {:answer/id #uuid "9415b7a9-5a1a-57a3-803c-ddf72eef3856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{k}{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541729} {:answer/id #uuid "bab4b395-1c32-5273-a354-4159ddc0b2ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541808} {:answer/id #uuid "dc7edd2e-8ea0-5615-81cd-8f820d79ec79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-k{h}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541892}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541499} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ef6a3fe-d635-518d-8afa-e08f3e08c478" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541987}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a29a3c61-aa9f-590b-908a-6227844310f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-119170" :question/problem "The volume of a cylinder decreases at a rate proportional to its volume. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the height, $h(t),$ in this process. \nAssume that the radius of the cylinder does not change and that $k>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of a cylinder **decreases** at a rate **proportional** to its **volume**. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a cylinder* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ *decreases* at a rate *proportional to the volume.* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kV,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a cylinder is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ and using the fact that the radius of the cylinder does not change, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-kV \\\\ \\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-k(\\pi {r}^{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{k\\pi {r}^{2}h}{\\pi {r}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-kh.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "53b991cc-3a22-5116-adab-cd17f3c705cb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542238}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542271 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542136 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1973d584-80ce-553d-a306-6d2016596bab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=K-r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541473} {:answer/id #uuid "4fb5aa8c-19fb-505d-99d0-6d1e5e391d3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-Kr" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541585} {:answer/id #uuid "86e0e1ed-497b-5bc3-9be6-1e1fb1057fc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=K{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541694} {:answer/id #uuid "a8cbfbe5-1faa-5f71-b7e8-8941f3018693" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-K{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541770} {:answer/id #uuid "feafdf56-0689-5b42-9564-9aa9c5d0dfa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{K}{r}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541957}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541585} :answer-field/id #uuid "60e99a5a-7fef-5572-9023-63b84b45c7f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3da31f55-8d6c-5a19-aa68-60451055a7de" :question/math-academy-id "q-119166" :question/problem "The volume of a cylinder decreases at a rate that's proportional to the square of its radius. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ in this process. Assume that the height of the cylinder does not change and that $K>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of a cylinder **decreases** at a rate that's **proportional** to the **square of its radius**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a cylinder* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ *decreases* at a rate that's *proportional to the square of its radius.* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-k{r}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a cylinder is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ and the fact that the height of the cylinder does not change, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\pi hr\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-k{r}^{2} \\\\ 2\\pi hr\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-k{r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{k{r}^{2}}{2\\pi hr} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{kr}{2\\pi h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{2\\pi h}>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-Kr.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "88e7fdbd-88d3-5a0c-b233-89990b64230f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542256}}] :activity/title "Further Modeling With First-Order ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541426}]}
